[ROCm] Emit occupancy from T.annotate_min_blocks_per_sm on HIP

The annotation reached only the CUDA backend, so HIP kernels could not ask the
compiler to keep registers low enough for more than one block per compute unit.
On gfx90a a 256x128x32 GEMM compiled with 143 VGPRs + 128 AGPRs (Occupancy 1),
which limits it to one block per compute unit whatever LDS allows.

HIP's second __launch_bounds__ argument is amdgpu_waves_per_eu, not blocks per
SM. Convert: ceil(blocks * ceil(threads / wave_size) / 4). The two only agree
for 256 threads on a 64-wide wavefront: 2 blocks of 128 threads need 1 wave per
SIMD, not 2. Without the annotation the emitted source does not change.

Measured on MI250X (gfx90a), ROCm 6.2.4, through the JIT: the same 256x128x32
kernel with the annotation at 2 compiles to 166 VGPRs + 0 AGPRs, Occupancy 3, no
scratch, and goes from 53 TF to 101 to 103 TF at 8192x8192x8192 and from 41 TF to
91 to 95 TF at 16384x16384x16384 (fp16, two allocations).

The attribute key moves from cuda/op/builtin.h to op/builtin.h, as both backends
now use it.

